Sarah Burton, Liam Sutton, and Eva Cronin
Lenoir-Rhyne University
BUS.446
Final Project Report
November 22, 2026
2
Abstract
This project examines factors associated with estimated annual revenue among Airbnb
listings in Asheville, North Carolina. The analysis uses a dataset of 2,865 listings and 90
variables obtained from Inside Airbnb. The project’s business objective is to identify property,
pricing, geographic, and host-related characteristics associated with stronger revenue
performance and to translate those patterns into practical considerations for property owners and
potential investors. The original analysis uses Tableau visualizations and a multiple regression
model. Descriptive findings indicate that larger entire-home listings generally have higher
median estimated annual revenue, although the pattern is not uniform across bedroom counts.
Location also matters: the project reports median annual revenue of $20,544 for ZIP code 28801
and $19,780 for ZIP code 28806, while ZIP code 28715 has the lowest reported median at
$11,970. The regression results show positive associations between estimated annual revenue
and nightly price, guest capacity, review score, and Superhost status. Bedroom count has a
negative coefficient when the other model variables are held constant. The model explains
approximately 33.2% of the variation in estimated annual revenue. These findings suggest that
investment decisions should consider multiple factors rather than rely on property size, location,
or nightly price alone. Because the outcome is estimated gross revenue rather than net profit, the
results should be used as planning evidence, not as a guarantee of financial performance.
Keywords: Airbnb, Asheville, estimated revenue, regression analysis, short-term rentals
