Factors Associated With Estimated Airbnb Revenue in Asheville, North Carolina
Introduction
Short-term rental platforms have created opportunities for property owners to make
residential and investment properties available to travelers. Airbnb listings differ in size,
location, nightly price, amenities, guest capacity, and host reputation. These differences make it
difficult for owners and potential investors to determine which property characteristics are most
closely associated with stronger financial performance. A property with more bedrooms may be
able to accommodate more guests, but it may also require a larger initial investment and higher
operating expenses. Likewise, a higher nightly price may increase potential revenue, but it does
not necessarily mean that a property will attract enough bookings to achieve that revenue.
This project focuses on the Asheville, North Carolina, Airbnb market. Asheville is the
geographic market represented in the provided listing dataset, and the analysis compares
estimated annual revenue across property sizes, ZIP codes, nightly prices, guest ratings, and host
categories. We are not trying to predict the exact return someone would earn by buying a
particular property. Instead, we use the listings to identify patterns that can help owners and
investors make more informed decisions about pricing, operations, and possible purchases.
Business Problem and Project Objective
The central business problem is determining which listing characteristics are associated
with higher estimated Airbnb revenue in Asheville. The project’s stated objective is to identify
the key factors associated with higher Airbnb revenue and provide data-driven recommendations
for property owners and potential investors (Burton et al., 2026). This objective is important
because investment decisions based on a single characteristic can be misleading. For example,
4
selecting a property solely because it has many bedrooms may overlook its location, nightly
price, guest capacity, customer experience, and operating costs.
The analysis addresses four related questions: (a) How does bedroom count relate to
estimated annual revenue? (b) How does revenue vary across Asheville ZIP codes? (c) What
relationship appears between nightly price and estimated annual revenue? (d) Are guest ratings
and Superhost status associated with revenue after accounting for other variables? Together,
these questions provide a broader view of the market than any one chart or measure could
provide.
