Dataset and Analytical Approach
Dataset Description
The supplied workbook contains 2,865 Airbnb listing records and 90 variables. The
project presentation identifies Inside Airbnb as the data source and describes the variables as
covering property characteristics, nightly pricing, geographic location, host information, and
customer reviews (Burton et al., 2026). The workbook includes fields such as room type,
property type, bedrooms, beds, bathrooms, guest capacity, price, availability, estimated
occupancy, estimated revenue, review scores, host experience, and Superhost status. The
workbook records a scrape date of September 19, 2026.
The key outcome variable is estimated annual revenue, represented in the workbook as
estimated_revenue_l365d. This measure is an estimate of listing revenue over the previous 365
days. It should not be interpreted as verified cash receipts or profit. The workbook also includes
estimated occupancy over the same period, nightly price information, and review measures. The
project presentation reports a dataset size of 2,865 listings; the number of usable observations
can differ by analysis because some fields contain missing values. For example, the workbook
has fewer nonmissing observations for estimated revenue and review scores than it has total
listing records.
Methods and Visualizations
The project uses Tableau to explore relationships among listing characteristics and
estimated revenue. The presentation includes a bedroom-count comparison for entire-home
listings, a ZIP-code revenue comparison, a geographic distribution map, a scatterplot of nightly
price and estimated annual revenue, a comparison of review ratings by host experience, a
6
Superhost comparison, and a multiple regression table (Burton et al., 2026). Each visualization
addresses a different part of the business problem.
The descriptive charts are useful for identifying broad patterns and unusual observations.
The regression analysis extends the descriptive work by estimating the association between
several explanatory variables and estimated annual revenue at the same time. The presentation
reports regression coefficients, p-values, and 95% confidence intervals. The report interprets
those coefficients as conditional associations: each coefficient describes the estimated
relationship with revenue when the other variables included in the model are held constant.
The available materials do not include a separate regression output file, full model
specification, or detailed documentation of all data-cleaning decisions. Accordingly, the
regression values in this report are reproduced from the project presentation rather than
independently re-estimated. This distinction is important when evaluating the precision and
reproducibility of the findings.
