Findings
Property Size and Estimated Revenue
For the bedroom comparison, we looked at entire-home listings. Overall, the median
estimated annual revenue tends to be higher for homes with more bedrooms. The reported
median revenue is $10,764 for studio-style listings with zero bedrooms, $17,790 for onebedroom listings, $20,196 for two-bedroom listings, $22,656 for three-bedroom listings, and
$29,250 for four-bedroom listings. The five-bedroom category has a reported median of $47,709.
This pattern supports the idea that larger homes may appeal to groups and families seeking more
space and may therefore have greater revenue potential.
The pattern is not consistent at every bedroom count. The presentation reports median
estimated annual revenue of $40,092 for six-bedroom listings, $99,387 for seven-bedroom
listings, $59,166 for eight-bedroom listings, and $30,879 for nine-bedroom listings. The sevenbedroom category has the highest reported median, but it contains only 20 listings. The eightbedroom group contains fewer listings, and the nine-bedroom group is smaller still. As a result,
the higher median for seven-bedroom properties should not be interpreted as proof that a sevenbedroom property will reliably outperform every other property size.
Property size is therefore a useful screening characteristic, but it is not sufficient for an
investment decision. Larger homes may have higher revenue potential because they can
accommodate more guests and may support higher nightly prices. However, they can also
require more capital, maintenance, cleaning, utilities, furnishings, and ongoing management. The
presentation appropriately cautions that higher revenue does not necessarily mean higher
profitability (Burton et al., 2026).
8
Location and Estimated Revenue
The ZIP-code chart shows that estimated annual revenue is not the same across all areas.
The presentation reports that ZIP code 28801 has the highest median at approximately $20,544,
followed by ZIP code 28806 at $19,780. ZIP codes 28804 and 28732 are also described as
relatively strong. ZIP code 28715 has the lowest reported median annual revenue at
approximately $11,970 (Burton et al., 2026).
These results suggest that location should be evaluated at a more detailed level than
simply deciding to invest somewhere in Asheville. Different areas may attract different types of
visitors, provide different access to attractions, or face different levels of competition. The
geographic distribution map also shows listing clusters throughout Asheville. Higher-revenue
listings appear in multiple areas rather than being concentrated in one single location. This
means that a ZIP-code median can help identify areas for further research, but it cannot replace
property-level due diligence.
9
Nightly Price and Estimated Annual Revenue
In the scatterplot from our presentation, listings with higher nightly prices generally also
show higher estimated annual revenue. Listings with higher nightly prices tend to have the
potential to generate more annual revenue. At the same time, most listings are clustered at lower
nightly prices, and the vertical spread of the points indicates substantial variation in revenue
among properties with similar prices. The chart also contains a small number of extreme
observations with unusually high nightly prices or estimated annual revenue (Burton et al.,
2026).
The visual pattern supports the idea that nightly price contributes to revenue, but it does
not establish that raising a price will automatically increase revenue. If a price is set too high
relative to comparable listings, booking demand may decline. Annual revenue reflects both the
amount charged and the number of nights booked. Owners should therefore consider local
competitors, seasonality, property capacity, availability, and guest demand when setting prices.
Customer Satisfaction and Host Experience
Our customer satisfaction chart shows average guest ratings staying close to 4.9 out of 5,
regardless of how long the host has been on Airbnb. The chart does not show a clear upward or
downward pattern as the number of years hosting increases. The project describes this as a
ceiling effect: because ratings are already clustered near the top of the scale, there is limited
room for visible variation (Burton et al., 2026).
This finding suggests that host tenure alone may not explain meaningful differences in
guest satisfaction in this dataset. Newer hosts can receive ratings similar to those of more
experienced hosts. However, the narrow range of ratings also limits the ability of this chart to
distinguish among listings. A high average rating is still important as an indicator of positive
10
guest experience, but the chart does not establish that every listing delivers the same level of
service or that host experience is irrelevant to operating performance.
Superhost Performance
We also compared Superhosts with other hosts to see how host status relates to review
ratings and estimated annual revenue. The visual suggests a difference between the two groups
and motivates the question of whether Superhost status provides a marginal revenue benefit. The
regression analysis provides a more specific estimate: after accounting for the other included
variables, Superhost listings are associated with approximately $16,372 more estimated annual
revenue. This is an association, not proof that obtaining Superhost status by itself causes revenue
to increase.
