Regression Analysis (Continued)
Review score has a positive coefficient of $17,015.87. A one-point increase in the review
score is associated with approximately $17,016 more estimated annual revenue when the other
variables are held constant. Its reported 95% confidence interval ranges from $9,372.32 to
$24,659.42. This result supports the importance of customer experience in the project’s revenue
analysis. However, review scores are bounded and concentrated near the top of the scale, so a
one-point change is substantial and should not be interpreted as a small, routine improvement.
Superhost status has a positive coefficient of $16,371.69, with a reported 95% confidence
interval from $14,005.52 to $18,737.85. This suggests that Superhost listings have higher
estimated annual revenue than comparable non-Superhost listings within the model’s adjustment
framework. Superhost status may reflect several underlying qualities, such as reliable hosting,
guest communication, and consistent service. The regression cannot isolate which of those
characteristics explains the association, and the result does not prove that the designation itself
causes the higher revenue.
Bedrooms has a negative coefficient of -$4,435.74. Holding nightly price, guest capacity,
review score, and Superhost status constant, each additional bedroom is associated with
approximately $4,436 less estimated annual revenue. This may appear to conflict with the
descriptive bedroom chart, which generally shows higher median revenue among larger entirehome listings. However, the two analyses answer different questions. The descriptive chart
compares median revenue across bedroom groups. The regression estimates the relationship with
bedrooms after statistically controlling for guest capacity and the other variables. Because
bedroom count and guest capacity overlap conceptually, the coefficient for bedrooms represents
14
the additional association of bedrooms among properties with the same modeled capacity and
other characteristics.
The negative bedroom coefficient should therefore not be interpreted as evidence that
adding bedrooms will automatically reduce revenue. It may reflect overlap among predictors,
differences in property mix, or other factors not represented in the model. The result is a reason
to avoid using bedroom count as a stand-alone investment rule.
Overall Model Fit
An R-squared of about .332 means that the model explains roughly 33.2% of the
differences in estimated annual revenue across the listings included in the analysis. The
remaining 66.8% is associated with factors not captured by the model, random variation,
measurement limitations, or other influences. Potentially relevant factors include seasonal
demand, exact location, amenities, cleaning and management costs, property condition, booking
restrictions, minimum-night requirements, competition, and the timing of guest reservations. The
model is useful for identifying associations, but it is not a complete forecasting system.
