Business Recommendations
Evaluate the Whole Property, Not Just Bedroom Count
Property owners and investors should consider larger properties, particularly those with
five to eight bedrooms, as potential opportunities for group-oriented demand. The reported
median revenue figures show that several of these categories have strong revenue potential.
Nevertheless, investors should compare the purchase price, renovation needs, furnishing costs,
taxes, insurance, utilities, cleaning, maintenance, and management expenses before deciding that
a larger property is financially attractive. Revenue is only one part of the investment equation.
Use Location as a Screening Tool
The reported ZIP-code results identify 28801 and 28806 as areas with the highest median
estimated annual revenue in the presentation. These areas may be useful starting points for
additional market research. Investors should still examine neighborhood-level competition,
nearby attractions, transportation, zoning and permitting requirements, and the characteristics of
comparable listings. The geographic map indicates that higher-revenue listings appear across
multiple clusters, so an investor should not assume that one ZIP code guarantees success.
Set Prices Competitively
The positive nightly-price coefficient suggests that pricing is an important revenue factor.
Owners should compare similar properties based on location, capacity, amenities, property type,
and guest appeal. Pricing should also respond to seasonal patterns and local demand. A strategy
that focuses only on charging the highest possible nightly rate may reduce bookings and
undermine annual revenue. Monitoring occupancy and revenue together is more informative than
monitoring nightly price alone.
16
Protect Guest Experience and Host Reputation
The positive associations for review score and Superhost status indicate that guest
experience and host reputation deserve ongoing attention. Owners should prioritize cleanliness,
accurate listing descriptions, clear check-in instructions, responsive communication, and prompt
resolution of guest concerns. The customer satisfaction chart shows that ratings remain high
across host experience levels, suggesting that newer hosts can also establish a strong reputation.
Consistent operating practices may help owners maintain positive reviews over time.
Make Decisions Using Multiple Measures
The final recommendation is to balance estimated revenue potential with occupancy,
property characteristics, location, competitive pricing, customer satisfaction, and operating costs.
Before purchasing or expanding a short-term rental, an investor should estimate net operating
income under conservative, expected, and optimistic booking scenarios. This additional financial
analysis is necessary because the project’s revenue measure does not subtract expenses or
establish profitability.
17
Limitations and Opportunities for Further Analysis
Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the findings. First, estimated
annual revenue is an estimate rather than audited financial information. The dataset does not
establish the actual expenses incurred by each host, so the analysis cannot determine net income,
cash flow, return on investment, or payback period. Second, the dataset is a snapshot of listings
collected on September 19, 2026. Availability, prices, listing status, and market conditions can
change over time. The results should not be assumed to represent every future season or market
condition.
Third, some variables contain missing values, and the number of observations used in
each chart or model may differ. The project presentation does not provide a complete account of
the sample size for every analysis or all data-cleaning procedures. Fourth, the regression model
explains approximately one-third of the variation in estimated annual revenue. Important
variables may be omitted, and relationships among predictors—particularly bedrooms and guest
capacity—may affect coefficient estimates. Finally, statistical association does not establish
causation. The results do not prove that changing one characteristic will produce the
corresponding change in revenue.
Future analysis could strengthen the project by documenting the number of observations
used in each model, testing for influential outliers, reviewing missing-data treatment, and
examining whether relationships differ by room type or property type. A future model could also
incorporate location controls, amenities, availability, occupancy, minimum-night rules, and
seasonal indicators. Most importantly, adding property acquisition costs and operating expenses
would allow the analysis to move from estimated gross revenue toward profitability and
investment return.
18
Conclusion
This project examined 2,865 Airbnb listings in Asheville, North Carolina, to identify
factors associated with estimated annual revenue. The descriptive analysis indicates that larger
entire-home listings generally have higher median revenue, although the pattern is not uniform
across bedroom counts. The ZIP-code comparison shows meaningful geographic differences,
with the presentation reporting the highest median revenue in ZIP code 28801 and the lowest in
ZIP code 28715. The nightly-price scatterplot suggests a positive relationship between price and
estimated revenue, while guest ratings remain close to 4.9 out of 5 across host experience levels.
The regression results add detail to these patterns. Nightly price, guest capacity, review
score, and Superhost status have positive associations with estimated annual revenue, while
bedroom count has a negative association after controlling for the other included variables. The
model explains approximately 33.2% of revenue variation, showing that multiple characteristics
matter and that additional influences remain unmeasured.
The central business takeaway is that successful Airbnb investment decisions require a
balanced evaluation. Property size, location, pricing, guest capacity, and customer satisfaction
can all inform a decision, but none should be used alone. Owners and investors should combine
market comparisons with a realistic assessment of expenses, occupancy, and regulatory
requirements. Used with these cautions, the analysis provides a practical starting point for
understanding revenue patterns in the Asheville short-term rental market.
